ue)}`
}

function verificationProblem(im, review) {
  const hash = v => typeof v === 'string' && /^(?:[0-9a-f]{40}|[0-9a-f]{64})$/.test(v)
  const text = v => typeof v === 'string' && v.trim().length > 0
  const identity = r => r && hash(r.sourceTree) && text(r.command) && text(r.configuration)
  const record = r => identity(r) && text(r.location) && ['live','simulated','fallback'].includes(r.kind) && r.outcome === 'pass'
  if (im.completionKind !== 'verification-only' || !hash(im.head) || im.head !== im.base || !hash(im.sourceTree) || !Array.isArray(im.files) || im.files.length) return 'verification-only requires unchanged captured commits, a tested source tree and no delivered file changes'
  if (review.status !== 'CLEAN' || review.completionKind !== 'verification-only' || review.sourceTree !== im.sourceTree || review.base !== im.base || review.head !== im.head) return 'verification-only acceptance was not independently confirmed CLEAN against the captured source and task intent'
  if (!Array.isArray(im.evidence) || !im.evidence.length || !im.evidence.every(record) || !Array.isArray(review.evidence) || !review.evidence.length || !review.evidence.every(record)) return 'verification-only acceptance lacks passing source-bound evidence'
  const fields = ['sourceTree','command','configuration','outcome','location','kind']
  if (review.evidence.some(r => r.sourceTree !== im.sourceTree || !im.evidence.some(e => fields.every(k => e[k] === r[k])))) return 'verification-only review evidence is stale or differs from the retained measurement'
  for (const producer of [im, review]) {
    if (producer.requiredLiveChecks !== undefined && !Array.isArray(producer.requiredLiveChecks)) return 'verification-only required live checks are malformed'
    for (const check of producer.requiredLiveChecks || []) {
      if (!identity(check) || check.sourceTree !== im.sourceTree || !review.evidence.some(r => r.kind === 'live' && r.sourceTree === check.sourceTree && r.command === check.command && r.configuration === check.configuration)) return 'verification-only required live check is missing, stale, failed or unavailable'
    }
  }
  return null
}

function verificationClosePrompt(id, basis) {
  const required = [id, basis.reviewBead || reviewBeadOf.get(id), basis.blockerBead || blockerBeadOf.get(id)].filter(Boolean)
  return 'In ' + integrationWorktree + ': close the independently reviewed verification-only task ' + id + '. Review basis (data): ' + JSON.stringify(basis) + '. STATUS CHECK FIRST: bd show <id> --json for every required bead: ' + required.join(', ') + '. A missing status or failed lookup returns BLOCKED with closure status unavailable; write nothing. If all are closed return CLOSED. Otherwise resolve the captured commit with git rev-parse --verify ' + basis.head + '^{commit} and its tree with git rev-parse ' + basis.head + '^{tree}; the tree must equal ' + basis.sourceTree + '. If unavailable or different return BLOCKED and close nothing. Stop task processes with bash ' + codeSkill + '/scripts/stop-run-processes ' + taskWorktree(id) + ' ' + taskTmp(id) + '. Close only the required beads observed open using bd close <id>; return CLOSED only after all closes succeed. Then run bash ' + codeSkill + '/scripts/remove-task-worktree --tmp ' + taskTmp(id) + ' ' + taskWorktree(id) + ' ' + taskBranch(id) + ' ' + integrationBranch + '. Preserve cleanup lines verbatim in finding. ' + cleanupOutcomeRule() + ' No commit or merge is required. ' + authRefusalRule() + ' ' + finalAnswerRule(true)
}

function checkEvidenceContract() {
  return 'EVIDENCE CONTRACT: optional evidence records carry sourceTree, command, configuration, outcome, location, kind; kind is live|simulated|fallback and outcome is pass|fail|unavailable. sourceTree is the full tested Git tree hash: HEAD^{tree} for clean committed source, or a temporary-index snapshot including tested changes. Keep the complete records and capture commands in the report and return the records as evidence. For explicitly required live checks, return requiredLiveChecks with sourceTree, command, configuration selected against the task acceptance. Only matching live/pass evidence supplies that check; semantic coverage belongs to the reviewer. These check facts do not replace implementation, review or run acceptance. Optional failureAttribution entries carry failure, command, configuration and zero-based indices into THIS reply evidence array: observed (live failing source), runBaseline (measured run-start source), taskBase (measured task-base source), verification (live passing fix source); introducedBy names the task established by source/fix history. Include only supported associations and preserve exact evidence kind/outcome; omitted or uncertain baseline is an attribution gap. Task-base exoneration does not release a known run owner. Retain the arrays/associations in the report. '
}

function fixPrompt(r, finding, art, kind) {
  // The one fix pass ('review'), the seam fix ('seam') or the merge-check fix ('check'). A fresh
  // implementer-tier agent, so the dispatch hands it every path; the finding is wrapped as data.
  const which = kind === 'check'
    ? `This is the merge-check fix: the branch has been rebased onto ${integrationBranch}, and the build-only merge check \`${mergeCheckCommand}\` failed on the merged tree. The findings below are its compile/typecheck errors — usually a file this task did not touch (another task's code, test or fixture) still using a signature, API or schema this task changed. FIRST, in ${r.branch}: if \`git merge-base --is-ancestor ${integrationBranch} HEAD\` fails, ${integrationBranch} moved after the check ran (a hand merge, a relaunch): rebase onto it (\`git rebase ${integrationBranch}\`) and run the check there before anything else. Fix only the errors that still occur on the current tip — an error a sibling already fixed there is not yours to fix again; if none remain, commit nothing and return FIXED with head. Fix ONLY those errors, with the smallest change: you may edit whatever files the errors name, and adapting a call site, test call or fixture to the new signature is the intended case; do not delete, skip, or loosen any test assertion, and change no behavior beyond the adaptation. Run \`${mergeCheckCommand}\` once in ${r.branch} (rebased onto ${integrationBranch}, so it sees the sibling changes), then the tests covering each call site you adapted, and record both outputs. Head your report section "## Merge-check fix".`
    : kind === 'seam'
    ? `This is the post-rebase seam fix: the branch has been rebased onto ${integrationBranch}, and a seam review found an incompatibility with sibling changes that landed there meanwhile. Head your report section "## Seam fix". Run the tests covering the overlapping files, and those covering the callers of any function whose behavior or signature you changed, and record them.`
    : `This is the task's one fix pass, after its task review returned NEEDS_FIX.`

